\paperw12240\paperh15840\margl1800\margr1800\margt1440\margb1440\f0\fs24\cf0
\pard\plain \sa180\sb180\ltrch\loch {I tugged on my usual brown work jacket, the one with yellow stains on the cuffs no matter how many times I scrubbed it, and stuffed my flat cap between the epaulettes on my shoulder. That trick I\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92d stolen from Alfred\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 my older brother\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 who always did the same with his beret when he was home on leave. He hadn\u8217\'92t been home in a while, but some habits linger, even in his absence.}
\par\plain {By the time I wheeled the old bicycle out of the shed, Mother was already waiting. She hitched her skirts up just enough to perch on the back seat, one hand on my shoulder, the other clutching her hat like the wind itself was trying to steal it from her.}
\par\plain {We rattled down the hill.}
\par\plain {The path from the Apothecary cut through waves of tall grass, bending and swaying with every gust of sea wind. From the corner of my eye, the fields looked less like earth and more like another ocean, one green tide chasing another, all whispering together in that endless, brushing sound. The air smelled of salt and iron, the kind that lived in your lungs and never left.}
\par\plain {Mother hunched forward against the gale, while I leaned into it. The cap stayed snug under my shoulder strap. Another small victory over nature.}
\par\plain {Welton\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s Apothecary shrank behind us on the cliff\u8217\'92s edge. It didn\u8217\'92t look like much now\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 just an old lighthouse, patched with brick and timber, its lantern long dead. But once upon a time, it had been the tallest thing for miles. Father bought it years ago, poured his fortune and his hands into reshaping it into a medicine brewery. He could have gone anywhere with his skill, joined the Alchemists\u8217\'92 Guild, chased glory in the cities. Instead, he brewed healing salves for Weshaven, sometimes for coin, often for nothing. Generous to a fault. Some people said he gave too much away. Others said he was stubborn. I think he just didn\u8217\'92t like seeing people suffer.}
\par\plain {The lighthouse had no ground for a proper road, only cliffs dropping sheer into the waves. So the car stayed downhill with the Franks, our neighbors in the stone cabin. It was an easy arrangement: we used their garage, they had free access to the car whenever they needed it. Not that they ever did\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 Mr. and Mrs. Franks were well into their seventies, and preferred walking to fussing with machines. The car was ours in all but name, gathering dust until moments like these.}
\par\plain {The car waited for us in the Franks\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92 garage, squatting in the dust like some old sea beast too stubborn to die. Teal paint clung in patches to its body, dulled by salt air, streaked with rust. The edges of the hood had the kind of rot you could put your finger through if you weren\u8217\'92t careful. Every bolt and seam seemed to whisper a warning: }{\f0\fs24\i1 don\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\af1\uc1\u8217\'92t trust me.}
\par\plain {Mother always called it \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93reliable.\u8221\'94 Personally, it gave me more anxiety than pride.}
\par\plain {Funnily enough, the car was hers, back from when she used to make deliveries in her younger days. It still smelled faintly of old herbs and sun-baked leather. I remembered the first time I drove it\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 clutch jerking, hands sweaty on the wheel\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 and how I clipped poor Mr. Simon\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s horse cart. The horse bolted like I\u8217\'92d personally insulted its lineage. Mr. Simon looked just as traumatized. I don\u8217\'92t think either of them ever forgave me.}
\par\plain {I slid into the driver\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s seat, the cracked vinyl creaking under my weight. Mother was still inside, speaking with the Franks. That left me and the beast to settle things between us.}
\par\plain {I turned the key. The engine coughed once, then gave nothing.}
\par\plain {I tried again. A groan, a wheeze, silence.}
\par\plain {My grip tightened. One more time.}
\par\plain {The engine sputtered, rattled, and with a final reluctant roar, caught. The whole frame shuddered like it wanted to shake itself apart, but it lived.}
\par\plain {\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Ha,\u8221\'94 I muttered, patting the dashboard. \u8220\'93Still got it.\u8221\'94}
